<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawit I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1382">1382</a>–<a href="/years/1413">1413</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawit I was an emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the late fourteenth and early fifteenth centuries. His campaigns against the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat Sultanate</a> led to the capture and execution of its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a> around 1410. This defeat drove the surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> princes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> and set the stage for the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Ifat, Sa'ad ad-Din II, had been defeated and killed near Zeila by the forces of the Ethiopian emperor, usually identified as <b>Dawit I</b>, in the early fifteenth century (the date is variously given between about 1403 and 1415), forcing his sons to flee across the Gulf of Aden.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the fifteenth century the emperor, probably <b>Dawit I</b>, gathered a large army, branded the Muslims of the region 'enemies of the Lord' and invaded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
